WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
The check is what makes delegation work.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:13.976
The Claude Code best-practices page puts it plainly: Claude stops when the work looks done, and if it has nothing to run that says whether the work actually is done, “looks done” is all it has.

3
00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:28.123
Give it something that comes back pass or fail (a test file, a build’s exit code, a linter, a script that diffs output against a fixture) and it can close the loop itself: change, check, read the failure, fix, check again.

4
00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:33.949
That’s the difference between an agent you supervise line by line and one you can leave for twenty minutes.

5
00:00:34.229 --> 00:00:40.608
Tests are the best general-purpose check because they encode the requirement in a form the machine can evaluate.

6
00:00:40.888 --> 00:00:44.109
That also makes them the most important thing to protect.

7
00:00:44.389 --> 00:00:50.085
If the agent can quietly change what the tests require, “all tests pass” stops meaning anything.

8
00:00:50.365 --> 00:00:58.557
This lesson covers both halves: using tests as the contract, and keeping the contract from being renegotiated by the party it binds.
